window.BLOG_POSTS=[{"slug":"ephemeralchat-real-time-ephemeral-chat-rooms-that-scale-across-any-instanc","title":"EphemeralChat - Real-Time Ephemeral Chat Rooms That Scale Across Any Instance","date":"2025-12-03","dateLabel":"Dec 3, 2025","topic":"Distributed Systems","tags":["system-design-concepts","software-engineering","system-design-interview","pub-sub","redis"],"excerpt":"🚀 EphemeralChat — Real-Time Ephemeral Chat Rooms That Scale Across Any Instance (Python + WebSockets + Redis Pub/Sub) EphemeralChat is my prototype for a high-performance real-time chat...","readMinutes":4,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/ephemeralchat-real-time-ephemeral-chat-rooms-that-scale-across-any-instance-fastapi-04acf03fe48a","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*vOe4p4fBUiSVRHhHQhcO-w.png","content":"<h3>🚀 EphemeralChat — Real-Time Ephemeral Chat Rooms That Scale Across Any Instance (Python + WebSockets + Redis Pub/Sub)<\/h3><p><strong>EphemeralChat<\/strong> is my prototype for a <strong>high-performance<\/strong> real-time chat system designed to be ephemeral and <strong>horizontally scalable<\/strong>.<br>Think <em>WhatsApp groups that self-destruct<\/em>, powered entirely by <strong>WebSockets<\/strong>, <strong>FastAPI<\/strong>, and <strong>Redis<\/strong> — and engineered so that a user can connect from <strong>any app instance<\/strong> and still join the same room. 🤝⚡<\/p><p>🌐 <strong>Live Demo:<\/strong> <a href=\"https://www.hansraj.me/ephemeralchat\">https://www.hansraj.me/ephemeralchat<\/a><br> 💻 <strong>Source Code:<\/strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/hansraj1999/EphemeralChat\">https://github.com/hansraj1999/EphemeralChat<\/a><\/p><p>In this write-up, I’ll break down the architecture, focusing on the coolest part:<\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*vOe4p4fBUiSVRHhHQhcO-w.png\" /><\/figure><blockquote><strong><em>WebSockets that remain fully synced even when users are distributed across multiple backend instances.<\/em><\/strong><em><br> No sticky sessions. No shared memory. Pure distributed magic. ✨<\/em><\/blockquote><h3>🧩 The Scaling Problem: WebSockets + Load Balancers<\/h3><p>WebSockets are stateful by nature… and load balancers hate state.<br> Suppose three users land on three different FastAPI instances:<\/p><pre>Client A —&gt; Instance 1  <br>Client B —&gt; Instance 2  <br>Client C —&gt; Instance 3<\/pre><p>Normally:<\/p><ul><li>A message sent from <strong>Instance 1<\/strong> <em>never<\/em> reaches users on Instances 2 &amp; 3<\/li><li>Each instance is isolated, unless you introduce a shared layer<\/li><li>Scaling becomes painful, brittle, and monolithic<\/li><\/ul><p>We want <strong>horizontal scaling<\/strong> → instances can come/go freely → and users can connect to <em>any<\/em> of them.<\/p><p>That’s where <strong>Redis Pub/Sub<\/strong> enters like a superhero 🦸‍♂️.<\/p><h3>🏗️ High-Level Architecture (Simple but Powerful)<\/h3><p>Here’s the mental model:<\/p><pre>Clients → Load Balancer → N FastAPI Instances → Redis (Data + Pub/Sub)<\/pre><p>Now visualize it with some life:<\/p><p>🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Users⬇ <br> 🎛️ Load Balancer (distributes WebSocket connections randomly)⬇<br> 🧱 Multiple FastAPI instances (stateless, independent)⬇<br> 🧠 Redis (global memory + broadcaster)<\/p><p>Every instance talks to Redis — <strong>not to each other<\/strong> — which keeps the architecture clean, scalable, and cloud-friendly.<\/p><h3>🔌 How WebSockets Stay Synced Across All Instances<\/h3><p>This is the heart of EphemeralChat ❤️‍🔥<br>Let’s break down the journey.<\/p><h3>🎯 Step 1: User Connects (From Any Instance!)<\/h3><p>When a user opens:<\/p><pre>wss://ephemeralchat.hansraj.me/rooms/{id}/ws<\/pre><p>What happens:<\/p><ol><li>Load balancer sends the request to a random FastAPI instance<\/li><li>Instance checks room metadata from Redis<\/li><li>Instance creates a unique connection_id<\/li><li>Adds WebSocket locally<\/li><li>And adds the user into Redis’ global tracking<\/li><\/ol><p>✔ Room occupancy<br> ✔ User presence<br> ✔ Expiry timers<br> ✔ Password checks<\/p><p>Everything is <strong>centralized in Redis<\/strong>, not in the instance.<\/p><h3>📡 Step 2: Each Instance Subscribes to Redis Pub/Sub<\/h3><p>Whenever an instance detects traffic for a room, it spawns a background listener:<\/p><pre>pubsub.subscribe(f&quot;room:channel:{room_id}&quot;)<\/pre><p>This is like saying:<\/p><blockquote><em>“Hey Redis, notify me whenever anyone publishes something in this room.”<\/em><\/blockquote><p>Now all instances are tuned to the same frequency 🔊📻<\/p><h3>💬 Step 3: Message Flow — The Scalable Magic<\/h3><p>Let’s say User A sends:<\/p><pre>{ &quot;type&quot;: &quot;message&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;hello!&quot; }<\/pre><p>On Instance 1:<\/p><ol><li>Message arrives<\/li><li>Instance validates &amp; enriches it<\/li><li>Publishes to Redis:<\/li><\/ol><pre>redis.publish(&quot;room:channel:{id}&quot;, json_payload)<\/pre><ol><li>Redis broadcasts it to <strong>all subscribers<\/strong><\/li><li>Every instance receives it<\/li><li>And forwards it to its local WebSocket clients 🎉<\/li><\/ol><p>Result?<\/p><blockquote><strong><em>One global chat room, served by many independent servers.<\/em><\/strong><\/blockquote><p>💥 No single point of failure<br> 💥 No sticky sessions<br> 💥 Ready for Kubernetes, ECS, Cloud Run or anything you throw at it<\/p><h3>⏳ Ephemeral Rooms — Powered by Redis TTL<\/h3><p>Rooms in EphemeralChat are <em>temporary by design<\/em>.<\/p><p>When a room is created:<\/p><p>🧾 room:meta:{id} → Redis Hash<br> ⏱ TTL applied (e.g., 10 minutes)<br> 👥 Users tracked in room:users:{id}<br> 📡 Messages broadcast via pub/sub<\/p><p>Redis automatically cleans up the room when TTL expires.<br> This makes ephemeral chat trivial, elegant, and cost-efficient.<\/p><h3>🧍‍♂️🧍 Presence, Online Users, and Room Details<\/h3><p>Since every user connection is stored in Redis:<\/p><ul><li>Total online users<\/li><li>User list<\/li><li>Remaining expiry time<\/li><li>Room slot availability<\/li><li>Whether the room requires a password<\/li><li>Owner presence<\/li><\/ul><p>All can be queried by <em>any instance<\/em>, anytime.<br> This gives the frontend rich real-time visibility without coupling.<\/p><h3>🐳 Deployment &amp; Infrastructure<\/h3><p>EphemeralChat is 100% containerized:<\/p><ul><li>🐍 <strong>FastAPI<\/strong> + Uvicorn<\/li><li>🔌 <strong>WebSockets<\/strong><\/li><li>🧠 <strong>Redis<\/strong><\/li><li>🐳 <strong>Docker + Docker Compose<\/strong><\/li><li>☁ Perfect for load-balanced, auto-scaled environments<\/li><\/ul><p>Scaling horizontally is as simple as:<\/p><pre>docker run ephemeralchat --replicas=10<\/pre><p>(or Kubernetes Deployment with 10 pods)<\/p><p>→ All 10 pods register with Redis<br> → Redis coordinates messages<br> → Load balancer sends traffic anywhere<br> → Everything just works 🤌<\/p><h3>🛠️ Ideal Use Cases<\/h3><p>EphemeralChat’s architecture is great if you need:<\/p><ul><li>🔥 Real-time messaging<\/li><li>🚀 Horizontal scaling<\/li><li>🧹 Auto-expiring rooms<\/li><li>🔐 Protected private sessions<\/li><li>🧭 Metadata accessible from every instance<\/li><li>🌀 Stateless compute nodes<\/li><\/ul><p>It’s a mini “distributed systems” lesson wrapped inside a fun chat application.<\/p>"},{"slug":"wherever-you-can-batch-always-batch","title":"Wherever You Can Batch, Always Batch","date":"2025-10-03","dateLabel":"Oct 3, 2025","topic":"Performance","tags":[],"excerpt":"Why Batching Matters The biggest enemies of performance are overhead and latency . Every time a system performs an operation like sending a network request, writing to a database, or read...","readMinutes":2,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/wherever-you-can-batch-always-batch-26e82ba5b7f9","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/0*tnEAJj9WB3gIdhhX","content":"<figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/0*tnEAJj9WB3gIdhhX\" /><\/figure><h3>Why Batching Matters<\/h3><p>The biggest enemies of performance are <strong>overhead<\/strong> and <strong>latency<\/strong>. Every time a system performs an operation like sending a network request, writing to a database, or reading from disk it pays a fixed “setup cost.”<\/p><p>Think of it like grocery shopping:<\/p><ul><li>Going to the store every time you need a single item wastes time and fuel.<\/li><li>Making a list, then buying everything in one trip, is batching.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Examples:<\/h3><h4>Database Inserts<\/h4><p>Imagine you need to insert 10,000 rows into a database.<\/p><ul><li>Dont insert one row at a time, Instead, if you batch 1,000 rows at a time, you only make 10 requests. The database processes them much faster because it can optimize disk writes internally.<\/li><\/ul><h4>Network Calls<\/h4><p>Every HTTP request carries overhead: DNS lookup, TLS handshake, request parsing, etc.<\/p><p>For example, if you need user details for 50 IDs:<\/p><ul><li>Making 50 separate API calls is slow and expensive.<\/li><li>A single endpoint like /users?ids=1,2,3,...50 can return everything in one go.<\/li><\/ul><h4>Disk I/O<\/h4><p>File systems also benefit from batching. Writing a log line to disk after every event causes frequent disk seeks and flushes.<br> Instead, systems like Kafka or databases buffer writes in memory and <strong>flush them in batches<\/strong>.<\/p><h4>Message Queues<\/h4><p>Even messaging systems like Kafka or RabbitMQ encourage batching. A producer doesn’t send every message immediately; it buffers them and sends a batch for higher throughput.<\/p><h3>The Trade-Off: Latency vs. Throughput<\/h3><p>Of course, batching comes with a trade-off. You wait to collect multiple items before processing them, which adds latency for individual operations.<\/p><blockquote>So the next time you’re building a system, ask yourself:<br> 👉 <em>Can I batch this?<\/em><\/blockquote>"},{"slug":"redis-pub-sub-local-memory-low-latency-high-consistency-caching","title":"Redis Pub/Sub + Local Memory = Low Latency, High Consistency Caching","date":"2025-09-03","dateLabel":"Sep 3, 2025","topic":"Caching","tags":["cache","caching","distributed-systems","redis","design-systems"],"excerpt":"Why not “just put it in Redis”? Round‑tripping to Redis is fast, but it’s still a network hop, it can add jitter, and you’ll eventually saturate your cache tier with read traffic. Keeping...","readMinutes":8,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/redis-pub-sub-local-memory-low-latency-high-consistency-caching-3740f66f0368","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1000/0*JuRAQyjH6WU8wXL_.jpeg","content":"<h3>Why not “just put it in Redis”?<\/h3><p>Round‑tripping to Redis is fast, but it’s still a network hop, it can add jitter, and you’ll eventually saturate your cache tier with read traffic. Keeping hot data in the <strong>same address space as your code<\/strong> removes the hop and cuts tail latency. The trick is <strong>keeping all those per‑instance caches in sync<\/strong>. That’s where Redis Pub/Sub shines not as a datastore, but as a <strong>broadcast mechanism<\/strong>.<\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1000/0*JuRAQyjH6WU8wXL_.jpeg\" /><\/figure><h3>The pattern in one sentence<\/h3><blockquote><strong><em>Use Redis only to broadcast small, versioned “set/delete” events; store the actual data in your app’s memory.<\/em><\/strong><\/blockquote><h3>Design goals<\/h3><ul><li><strong>Local reads<\/strong>: zero network for cache hits.<\/li><li><strong>High consistency<\/strong>: conflicts resolved by <strong>monotonic versions<\/strong>; apply‑only‑if‑newer semantics.<\/li><li><strong>Simple mental model<\/strong>: boring JSON messages on a single Redis channel per domain (e.g., cache:users).<\/li><li><strong>Resilience<\/strong>: handle missed messages with TTL self‑healing and read‑through refresh.<\/li><\/ul><h3>How it fits together<\/h3><pre>   ┌──────────────┐   publish {&quot;op&quot;:&quot;set&quot;,&quot;key&quot;,&quot;version&quot;,...}<br>   │   Database   │ &lt;--------------------------------------------┐<br>   └──────┬───────┘                                              │<br>          │ write                                                │<br>┌─────────▼─────────┐                                    ┌───────▼────────┐<br>│  Service (A)      │  &lt;== Redis Pub/Sub:cache:users ==&gt; │  Service (B)   │<br>│  in-proc cache    │                                    │  in-proc cache │<br>└────────┬──────────┘                                    └────────┬───────┘<br>         │  read (local)                            read (local)  │<br>         ▼                                                        ▼<br>     microseconds                                           microseconds<\/pre><h3>Message schema<\/h3><p>We’ll broadcast a JSON message:<\/p><pre>{<br>  &quot;op&quot;: &quot;set&quot; | &quot;del&quot;,<br>  &quot;key&quot;: &quot;user:42&quot;,<br>  &quot;version&quot;: 17,<br>  &quot;value&quot;: { ... only for &quot;set&quot; ... },<br>  &quot;sender&quot;: &quot;instance-abc&quot;,<br>  &quot;ts&quot;: 1730585582.123<br>}<\/pre><ul><li><strong>version<\/strong> is <em>monotonic per key<\/em>. Only apply a message if its version is <strong>strictly greater<\/strong> than what you currently hold.<\/li><li><strong>value<\/strong> is optional if you prefer pure invalidation. I usually include the value for instant warm‑ups.<\/li><li><strong>sender<\/strong> lets you ignore your own echo if you want (not required).<\/li><\/ul><p>Where does version come from?<\/p><ul><li><strong>Best<\/strong>: a version column in your DB row (e.g., integer bump on each write).<\/li><li><strong>Also fine<\/strong>: an atomic INCR in Redis per key (writes are far less frequent than reads).<\/li><\/ul><h3>The Python implementation (async, redis-py)<\/h3><blockquote><strong><em>What you get below<\/em><\/strong><em>: a minimal, production‑shaped example you can drop into a service. It uses <\/em><em>redis.asyncio, keeps a local dict with TTL + versioning, and streams Pub/Sub updates.<\/em><\/blockquote><blockquote><em>Requires: <\/em><em>pip install redis (v5+), Python 3.10+<\/em><\/blockquote><pre>import asyncio<br>import json<br>import os<br>import time<br>import uuid<br>from dataclasses import dataclass<br>from typing import Any, Dict, Optional, Callable<br>import redis.asyncio as redis  # pip install redis<br><br>REDIS_URL = os.getenv(&quot;REDIS_URL&quot;, &quot;redis://localhost:6379/0&quot;)<br>CACHE_CHANNEL = os.getenv(&quot;CACHE_CHANNEL&quot;, &quot;cache:users&quot;)<br>DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS = int(os.getenv(&quot;CACHE_TTL&quot;, &quot;60&quot;))<br>INSTANCE_ID = os.getenv(&quot;INSTANCE_ID&quot;, str(uuid.uuid4())[:8])<br><br># -----------------------------<br># Fake authoritative store (replace with your DB)<br># -----------------------------<br>class FakeDB:<br>    &quot;&quot;&quot;Simulates a DB with per-key versioning.&quot;&quot;&quot;<br>    def __init__(self):<br>        self._data: Dict[str, Any] = {}<br>        self._version: Dict[str, int] = {}<br>        self._lock = asyncio.Lock()<br>    async def read(self, key: str) -&gt; Optional[tuple[Any, int]]:<br>        # simulate I/O latency<br>        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)<br>        if key not in self._data:<br>            return None<br>        return self._data[key], self._version.get(key, 0)<br>    async def write(self, key: str, value: Any) -&gt; int:<br>        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)<br>        async with self._lock:<br>            v = self._version.get(key, 0) + 1<br>            self._version[key] = v<br>            self._data[key] = value<br>            return v<br>    async def delete(self, key: str) -&gt; int:<br>        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)<br>        async with self._lock:<br>            v = self._version.get(key, 0) + 1<br>            self._version[key] = v<br>            self._data.pop(key, None)<br>            return v<br><br># -----------------------------<br># Local cache with version + TTL<br># -----------------------------<br>@dataclass<br>class CacheItem:<br>    value: Any<br>    version: int<br>    expires_at: float<br>class LocalCache:<br>    def __init__(self, ttl_seconds: int = DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS):<br>        self._items: Dict[str, CacheItem] = {}<br>        self.ttl = ttl_seconds<br>    def get(self, key: str) -&gt; Optional[Any]:<br>        item = self._items.get(key)<br>        if not item:<br>            return None<br>        if item.expires_at &lt; time.time():<br>            # expired; drop and miss<br>            self._items.pop(key, None)<br>            return None<br>        return item.value<br>    def get_version(self, key: str) -&gt; int:<br>        item = self._items.get(key)<br>        return item.version if item else 0<br>    def set_local(self, key: str, value: Any, version: int) -&gt; None:<br>        # Only accept monotonic increase<br>        cur = self._items.get(key)<br>        if cur and version &lt;= cur.version:<br>            return<br>        self._items[key] = CacheItem(value=value, version=version,<br>                                     expires_at=time.time() + self.ttl)<br>    def delete_local(self, key: str, version: int) -&gt; None:<br>        cur = self._items.get(key)<br>        if cur and version &lt;= cur.version:<br>            return<br>        self._items.pop(key, None)<br># -----------------------------<br># Pub/Sub client<br># -----------------------------<br>class CacheBus:<br>    def __init__(self, redis_url: str, channel: str, instance_id: str):<br>        self._r = redis.Redis.from_url(redis_url, decode_responses=False)<br>        self.channel = channel<br>        self.instance_id = instance_id<br>    async def publish_set(self, key: str, version: int, value: Any) -&gt; None:<br>        payload = json.dumps({<br>            &quot;op&quot;: &quot;set&quot;,<br>            &quot;key&quot;: key,<br>            &quot;version&quot;: version,<br>            &quot;value&quot;: value,<br>            &quot;sender&quot;: self.instance_id,<br>            &quot;ts&quot;: time.time(),<br>        })<br>        await self._r.publish(self.channel, payload)<br>    async def publish_del(self, key: str, version: int) -&gt; None:<br>        payload = json.dumps({<br>            &quot;op&quot;: &quot;del&quot;,<br>            &quot;key&quot;: key,<br>            &quot;version&quot;: version,<br>            &quot;sender&quot;: self.instance_id,<br>            &quot;ts&quot;: time.time(),<br>        })<br>        await self._r.publish(self.channel, payload)<br>    async def subscribe(self, handler: Callable[[dict], None]) -&gt; None:<br>        pubsub = self._r.pubsub()<br>        await pubsub.subscribe(self.channel)<br>        async for msg in pubsub.listen():<br>            if msg is None or msg.get(&quot;type&quot;) != &quot;message&quot;:<br>                continue<br>            # msg[&#39;data&#39;] is bytes when decode_responses=False<br>            try:<br>                data = json.loads(msg[&quot;data&quot;].decode(&quot;utf-8&quot;))<br>            except Exception:<br>                continue<br>            handler(data)<br># -----------------------------<br># Cache‑backed service facade<br># -----------------------------<br>class UserCacheService:<br>    def __init__(self, db: FakeDB, cache: LocalCache, bus: CacheBus):<br>        self.db = db<br>        self.cache = cache<br>        self.bus = bus<br>    async def get_user(self, user_id: str) -&gt; Optional[dict]:<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;Read‑through: prefer local; on miss, load from DB and set local (no publish).&quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        key = f&quot;user:{user_id}&quot;<br>        v = self.cache.get(key)<br>        if v is not None:<br>            return v<br>        row = await self.db.read(key)<br>        if not row:<br>            return None<br>        value, version = row<br>        self.cache.set_local(key, value, version)<br>        return value<br>    async def update_user(self, user_id: str, new_value: dict) -&gt; dict:<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        Write‑through: write DB first (source of truth), then publish and set local.<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        key = f&quot;user:{user_id}&quot;<br>        version = await self.db.write(key, new_value)<br>        # Update local immediately to avoid self‑miss<br>        self.cache.set_local(key, new_value, version)<br>        # Broadcast to other instances<br>        await self.bus.publish_set(key, version, new_value)<br>        return {&quot;ok&quot;: True, &quot;version&quot;: version}<br>    async def delete_user(self, user_id: str) -&gt; dict:<br>        key = f&quot;user:{user_id}&quot;<br>        version = await self.db.delete(key)<br>        self.cache.delete_local(key, version)<br>        await self.bus.publish_del(key, version)<br>        return {&quot;ok&quot;: True, &quot;version&quot;: version}<br>    # Handler for remote messages<br>    def apply_remote(self, message: dict) -&gt; None:<br>        # Optional: ignore the echo of our own messages<br>        if message.get(&quot;sender&quot;) == INSTANCE_ID:<br>            return<br>        op = message.get(&quot;op&quot;)<br>        key = message.get(&quot;key&quot;)<br>        version = int(message.get(&quot;version&quot;, 0))<br>        if op == &quot;set&quot;:<br>            value = message.get(&quot;value&quot;)<br>            self.cache.set_local(key, value, version)<br>        elif op == &quot;del&quot;:<br>            self.cache.delete_local(key, version)<br># -----------------------------<br># Demo / manual test<br># -----------------------------<br>async def main():<br>    print(f&quot;[{INSTANCE_ID}] starting… channel={CACHE_CHANNEL}&quot;)<br>    db = FakeDB()<br>    cache = LocalCache(ttl_seconds=DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS)<br>    bus = CacheBus(REDIS_URL, CACHE_CHANNEL, INSTANCE_ID)<br>    svc = UserCacheService(db, cache, bus)<br>    # Start the subscriber loop in background<br>    sub_task = asyncio.create_task(bus.subscribe(svc.apply_remote))<br>    # Seed a user (simulate admin tool writing the DB)<br>    await svc.update_user(&quot;42&quot;, {&quot;id&quot;: 42, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;role&quot;: &quot;admin&quot;})<br>    # Local read (cache hit after first fetch)<br>    print(&quot;First read (may DB‑hit):&quot;, await svc.get_user(&quot;42&quot;))<br>    print(&quot;Second read (local):    &quot;, await svc.get_user(&quot;42&quot;))<br>    # Update from *this* instance<br>    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)<br>    await svc.update_user(&quot;42&quot;, {&quot;id&quot;: 42, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ada Lovelace&quot;, &quot;role&quot;: &quot;admin&quot;})<br>    # Simulate time passing<br>    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)<br>    print(&quot;Read after update (local, new):&quot;, await svc.get_user(&quot;42&quot;))<br>    # Keep process alive to listen for remote updates<br>    # Tip: run two shells with different INSTANCE_IDs to see cross‑process updates.<br>    await asyncio.sleep(3600)<br>    sub_task.cancel()<br>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:<br>    try:<br>        asyncio.run(main())<br>    except KeyboardInterrupt:<br>        pass<\/pre><p><strong>How to try this locally<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Start Redis (docker run -p 6379:6379 redis).<\/li><li>Open two terminals:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Terminal A: INSTANCE_ID=A python app.py<\/li><li>Terminal B: INSTANCE_ID=B python app.py<\/li><\/ul><p>3. In terminal A, type Ctrl+C after it prints the first lines, then tweak the code to call update_user with different values, or just let both run and observe B receive updates from A via Pub/Sub. (In a real service, different processes would be handling real requests.)<\/p><h3>Consistency<\/h3><ul><li><strong>Monotonic versions<\/strong>: Every message carries a per‑key version. Your local cache updates <strong>only<\/strong> when incoming.version &gt; local.version. That alone kills most common races.<\/li><li><strong>Publish after commit<\/strong>: Always <em>commit to your DB first<\/em>, then publish. Consumers who miss the message will still get the right value on a later read‑through hit.<\/li><li><strong>Self‑healing TTL<\/strong>: Add a short TTL (30–120s). If any instance misses a Pub/Sub message, the next read after expiry does a read‑through and corrects itself. (You can set TTLs longer for very hot keys.)<\/li><li><strong>Cold starts<\/strong>: On boot, the first read will prime the cache from the DB; you don’t need a bulk warm‑up unless you want one.<\/li><\/ul><blockquote><strong><em>What if Pub/Sub drops messages?<\/em><\/strong><em><br> It can, because Pub/Sub is fire‑and‑forget. For <\/em>critical<em> keys, consider either:<\/em><\/blockquote><ul><li>A periodic “version check” (e.g., compare DB version on hit every N seconds), or<\/li><li>A fallback replay channel using <strong>Redis Streams<\/strong> (durable, acked). The rest of the design stays the same.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Why this is “highly consistent” in practice<\/h3><ul><li>Writes are serialized by your DB (or any authoritative store). The version comes from <em>there<\/em> (or a single atomic counter), which gives you a <strong>total order per key<\/strong>.<\/li><li>All instances apply updates in the same order (by version), so given message delivery within a DC and modest TTLs, caches converge quickly.<\/li><li>The read path is deterministic: hit local if fresh; otherwise fetch from truth and set local.<\/li><\/ul><p>If you need hard guarantees like linearizability under cross‑region partitions, you’re outside the scope of a simple cache and into the land of CRDTs, consensus, or per‑request “read your writes” tokens. For most web backends, the pattern above is the sweet spot.<\/p><h3>Operational checklist<\/h3><ul><li><strong>Channels<\/strong>: use one channel per domain (cache:users, cache:products) to keep messages small and consumers focused.<\/li><li><strong>Payloads<\/strong>: keep JSON tiny; include only what’s necessary. If values are bulky, send invalidations only (op:&quot;del&quot;) and let readers fetch on demand.<\/li><li><strong>Backpressure<\/strong>: Pub/Sub buffers in clients; ensure your subscriber loop is async and fast (as in the sample).<\/li><li><strong>Health<\/strong>: increment a counter on applied messages; alert if an instance stops receiving updates.<\/li><li><strong>Memory limits<\/strong>: add a max‑size policy (evict oldest) if you cache many keys; the example uses TTLs, but you can wrap an LRU around it.<\/li><li><strong>Testing<\/strong>: chaos‑test by randomly dropping messages and verifying TTL self‑healing + read‑through correctness.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Variations you might prefer<\/h3><ul><li><strong>Write‑behind<\/strong>: For some workloads, you can publish first, then write DB, but you’ll need fencing tokens to avoid lost updates. I don’t recommend this for beginners.<\/li><li><strong>No value in messages<\/strong>: Send only invalidations (op:&quot;del&quot;) if you’re sensitive to payload size or PII in transit. It adds a DB read on the next miss but keeps the bus clean.<\/li><li><strong>Streams for durability<\/strong>: Swap Pub/Sub with Redis Streams if you need at‑least‑once delivery + replay on subscriber restart.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Closing thoughts<\/h3><p>I like this pattern because it’s <strong>boring<\/strong> and <strong>predictable<\/strong>. It leans on primitives we already trust:<\/p><ul><li>Your DB is the source of truth.<\/li><li>Redis is a fast broadcast bus, not a second datastore.<\/li><li>Your application gets <strong>local, predictable read latency<\/strong> and scales reads linearly as you add instances.<\/li><\/ul><blockquote>Remember: “Redis is not just a cache storage”<\/blockquote>"},{"slug":"ever-wondered-how-database-connection-pools-are-created-and-maintained","title":"Ever wondered how database connection pools are created and maintained?","date":"2024-12-15","dateLabel":"Dec 15, 2024","topic":"Databases","tags":["asynchronous","connection-pool","python","mysql","database"],"excerpt":"Ever wondered how Database connection pools are created and maintained? Background: Let’s say you want to query the database and to do that we first have to establish a connection between...","readMinutes":3,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/ever-wondered-how-database-connection-pools-are-created-and-maintained-930ca5f9f946","image":"","content":"<h3>Ever wondered how Database connection pools are created and maintained?<\/h3><p><strong>Background:<\/strong><\/p><p>Let’s say you want to query the database and to do that we first have to establish a connection between your service’s API gateway and Database’s API gateway now this connection is a TCP connection which involves 3 way handshake.<\/p><p>So if you create a connection for every request and then close it this will add unnecessary latency and will burn resources to process that request.<\/p><p>Instead we create a connection pool, now what exactly is a connection pool?<\/p><p>It is nothing but pre-created connections of the databases so you don’t have to create and close connections for every request. Let’s say you create a pool of 10 and 11 concurrent requests gets to our api the 11th or any subsequent request will be queued till a connection comes back in the pool.<\/p><p>Now this connection pooling is supported by mostly every database drivers<\/p><p>Instead of relying on pre-built solutions, I decided to go deeper — <strong>building my own custom MySQL connection pool<\/strong> from scratch! 💻<\/p><p><strong>Why? How?<\/strong><br> Connection pools improve database efficiency by reusing connections instead of constantly opening and closing them.<\/p><p>But have you ever thought about:<br>👉 <strong>How connections are reused?<\/strong><br>👉 <strong>How threads and async tasks are managed?<\/strong><\/p><p>These questions inspired me to build a <strong>Custom MySQL Connection Pool<\/strong> with two approaches:<\/p><ul><li><strong>🧵 Threaded Pool<\/strong> — Built with Python’s threading for concurrency.<\/li><li><strong>⚙️ Asynchronous Pool<\/strong> — Fully async using async/await to avoid blocking operations.<\/li><\/ul><p>In this blog will go with async pool management and you can find threaded one in my GitHub.<\/p><pre>import mysql.connector<br>from helper import get_mysql_connection<br>import asyncio<br>import time<br><br>class ConnectionPool:<br>  connections = []<br>  def __init__(self, min_pool_size, max_pool_size):<br>    for i in range(min_pool_size):<br>    self.connections.append(get_mysql_connection())<br>    self.min_pool_size = min_pool_size<br>    self.max_pool_size = max_pool_size<br><br>  async def get_connection(self):<br>    while not self.connections:<br>      await asyncio.sleep(1)<br>    print(&quot;remaining useable connections in pool&quot;, len(self.connections) - 1)<br>    return self.connections.pop()<br><br>  async def return_connection(self, connection):<br>    print(&quot;remaining useable connections in pool&quot;, len(self.connections) + 1)<br>    self.connections.append(connection)<br><br>  def close_connections(self):<br>    for connection in self.connections:<br>    connection.close()<br>    self.connections = []<\/pre><p>In the code above:<\/p><ul><li><strong>get_connection<\/strong>: Retrieves a connection from the pool. If no connection is available, it creates new ones (up to the maximum pool size).<\/li><li><strong>return_connection<\/strong>: Returns the connection back to the pool for reuse.<\/li><li><strong>close_connections<\/strong>: Closes all open connections when you’re done.<\/li><\/ul><p>To test the performance of the custom connection pool, we can create some simulated database queries:<\/p><pre>async def test_custom_async_connection_pool():<br>  async_connection_pool = ConnectionPool(10, 20)<br>  async def _benchmark_with_custom_async_threaded_connection_pool():<br>    new_connection = await async_connection_pool.get_connection()<br>    new_connection.ping()<br>    await async_connection_pool.return_connection(new_connection)<br>  start_time = time.time()<br>  tasks = []<br>  for i in range(int(100) + 1):<br>    tasks.append(_benchmark_with_custom_async_threaded_connection_pool())<br>  await asyncio.gather(*tasks)<br>  total_time_took = time.time() - start_time<br>  output = {<br>    &quot;time_took&quot;: total_time_took,<br>    &quot;range&quot;: 100,<br>    &quot;bench_type&quot;: &quot;benchmark_with_custom_async_connection_pool&quot;,<br>    }<br>  print(output)<br>  return output<\/pre><p>If you’re curious to see the <strong>full implementation, live benchmarks, and step-by-step code<\/strong>, I’ve shared everything on GitHub:<br>👉 [<a href=\"https://github.com/hansraj1999/custom-mysql-connection-pool-implementation\">https://github.com/hansraj1999/custom-mysql-connection-pool-implementation<\/a>]<\/p><p>💡 <strong>Want to learn how to build your own connection pool?<\/strong><br>If you’d like a more detailed breakdown (or a step-by-step guide), drop a comment below, and I’ll be happy to share my thought process, challenges, and solutions!<\/p>"},{"slug":"scaling-databases-with-consistent-hashing-a-comprehensive-guide","title":"Scaling Databases with Consistent Hashing: A Comprehensive Guide","date":"2024-12-10","dateLabel":"Dec 10, 2024","topic":"Distributed Systems","tags":["system-design-concepts","consistent-hashing","backend","backend-development","database"],"excerpt":"Let’s say we have billions of records in our database. Despite adding indexes and caching frequent queries, the data size is still too large to be queried efficiently. To address this, we...","readMinutes":6,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/scaling-databases-with-consistent-hashing-a-comprehensive-guide-4904bb3ccc85","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*UOmLAaMDtx1fO6EYTtpJUg.png","content":"<p>Let’s say we have billions of records in our database. Despite adding indexes and caching frequent queries, the data size is still too large to be queried efficiently. To address this, we decide to distribute (shard) the data across two different nodes.<\/p><p>To achieve this, we hash the key, compute its modulo with the number of nodes, and store the data in the corresponding node. Queries are then routed to the appropriate node using the same logic. However, a problem arises when we either want to add or delete a node. In such cases, we need to move almost all the data, which is very expensive.<\/p><p>To minimize (though not eliminate) this cost, we use consistent hashing. It’s important to note that consistent hashing is not a magic solution and doesn’t completely solve the problem, contrary to what many internet blogs claim. Instead, it significantly reduces the amount of data that needs to be shifted when adding or removing a database node.<\/p><p>Despite its limitations, consistent hashing provides substantial benefits by minimizing record movement. How it achieves this will be discussed in detail, along with an implementation example.<\/p><h3>Without Consistent Hashing<\/h3><p>In traditional sharding, the system maps keys to specific nodes using a simple hashing technique. For example:<\/p><pre># Traditional Sharding <br>number_of_nodes = 3 <br>key = &quot;user1&quot; <br>node = hash(key) % number_of_nodes <br>print(f&quot;Data for {key} goes to Node {node}&quot;)<\/pre><p>This works well when the number of nodes is fixed. However, problems arise when we add or remove a node. For instance:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Adding a Node<\/strong>: If we increase the node count from 3 to 4, the modulo operation changes, causing nearly all keys to be remapped.<\/li><li><strong>Removing a Node<\/strong>: Similar to adding a node, removing one also leads to a complete redistribution of keys.<\/li><\/ul><p>Such massive data movement is costly and can disrupt system performance.<\/p><h3>Enter Consistent Hashing<\/h3><p><strong>Consistent hashing<\/strong> addresses this problem by ensuring that only a subset of keys gets remapped when nodes are added or removed. It organizes the hash space into a circular ring and maps nodes to points on this ring. Keys are then assigned to the next clockwise node on the ring.<\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*UOmLAaMDtx1fO6EYTtpJUg.png\" /><\/figure><h4>How It Works<\/h4><ol><li><strong>Node Placement<\/strong>: Nodes are hashed and placed on the ring.<\/li><li><strong>Key Assignment<\/strong>: Keys are hashed and assigned to the first node encountered while moving clockwise around the ring.<\/li><li><strong>Node Addition/Removal<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>When a node is <strong>added<\/strong>, only the keys between the new node and its predecessor need to be remapped.<\/li><li>When a node is <strong>removed<\/strong>, its keys are redistributed to its neighbors.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Practical Example: Adding and Removing Nodes<\/h3><h4>Initial Setup<\/h4><p><strong>Imagine a ring with three nodes: <\/strong><strong>db1, <\/strong><strong>db2, and <\/strong><strong>db3.<\/strong><\/p><pre>Ring: [db1] -&gt; [db2] -&gt; [db3]<\/pre><p>We will now distribute some keys to these nodes. Let’s say we have the following keys: user1, user2, user3.<br>We assign these keys to nodes using consistent hashing, with each key being hashed and assigned to the first node it encounters in the clockwise direction.<\/p><p><strong>Key Hashing and Assignment<\/strong><\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*7TuYYt4Tvk4m-dM6KK6cvQ.png\" /><\/figure><p>For simplicity, let’s assume the hash values for the keys are as follows (these are simplified examples; in real applications, you would use a cryptographic hash function like SHA-256 to get these values):<\/p><ul><li>Hash of user1: 10<\/li><li>Hash of user2: 30<\/li><li>Hash of user3: 70<\/li><\/ul><p>Now, based on these hashes, we assign the keys to nodes:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user1 (Hash: 10)<\/strong>:<br>The hash value 10 is placed before db2 on the ring. So, user1 will be assigned to db1 (the first node encountered clockwise from the hash position).<\/li><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user2 (Hash: 30)<\/strong>:<br>The hash value 30 is placed between db2 and db3 on the ring. So, user2 will be assigned to db2.<\/li><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user3 (Hash: 70)<\/strong>:<br>The hash value 70 is placed between db3 and db1 on the ring. So, user3 will be assigned to db3.<\/li><\/ol><pre>Initial Distribution: <br>- user1 -&gt; db1<br>- user2 -&gt; db2<br>- user3 -&gt; db3<\/pre><h4>Adding a Node (db4)<\/h4><p><strong>When <\/strong><strong>db4 is added, only the keys between <\/strong><strong>db3 and <\/strong><strong>db4 are remapped to <\/strong><strong>db4.<\/strong><\/p><pre>New Ring: [db1] -&gt; [db2] -&gt; [db3] -&gt; [db4]<\/pre><p><strong>Key Hashing and Assignment<\/strong><\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*LiN-Hd4KCb3Rf-24h2rmug.png\" /><\/figure><p>Now, when db4 is added, only the keys between db3 and db4 need to be remapped to db4. Let&#39;s check the hash positions of the existing keys:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user3 (Hash: 70)<\/strong>:<br>The hash value 70 lies between db3 and db1, so it will remain with db3.<\/li><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user1 (Hash: 10)<\/strong>:<br>The hash value 10 still lies before db2, so it stays with db1.<\/li><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user2 (Hash: 30)<\/strong>:<br>The hash value 30 lies between db2 and db3. Since db4 is between db3 and db2, user2 stays with db2.<\/li><\/ul><p>Now, no keys are remapped because the hash positions of existing keys don’t fall between db3 and db4. However, if there were any keys between db3 and db4, those keys would now be reassigned to db4.<\/p><pre>Redistribution: <br>- user1 -&gt; db1<br>- user2 -&gt; db2<br>- user3 -&gt; db3<\/pre><h4>Removing a Node (db2)<\/h4><p><strong>When <\/strong><strong>db2 is removed, its keys are redistributed to <\/strong><strong>db3.<\/strong><\/p><pre>New Ring: [db1] -&gt; [db3] -&gt; [db4]<\/pre><p><strong>Key Hashing and Assignment<\/strong><\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/1*GnFsPGjBhQ7TU905IIGWxg.png\" /><\/figure><p>we need to redistribute the keys that were assigned to it to its neighboring nodes.<br>Let’s go step-by-step:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Key <\/strong><strong>user2 (Hash: 30)<\/strong>:<br>Since user2 was assigned to db2, we will reassign it to db3 (the next node in the clockwise direction).<\/li><\/ol><p>After removing db2, the new ring looks like this:<br>Now, user2 is reassigned to db3.<\/p><h3>Python Implementation of Consistent Hashing<\/h3><h4>Let’s implement consistent hashing in Python:<\/h4><pre>import hashlib<br>from sortedcontainers import SortedDict<br>class ConsistentHashing:<br>    def __init__(self, replicas=3):<br>        self.replicas = replicas  # nodes<br>        self.ring = SortedDict()<br>        self.nodes = set()<br>    def _hash(self, key):<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;Hashes a key using SHA-256.&quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        return int(hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) # can use md5 as well.<br>    def add_node(self, node):<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;Adds a node to the ring.&quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        self.nodes.add(node)<br>        for i in range(self.replicas):<br>            virtual_node = f&quot;{node}:{i}&quot;<br>            self.ring[self._hash(virtual_node)] = node<br>    def remove_node(self, node):<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;Removes a node from the ring.&quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        self.nodes.discard(node)<br>        for i in range(self.replicas):<br>            virtual_node = f&quot;{node}:{i}&quot;<br>            del self.ring[self._hash(virtual_node)]<br>    def get_node(self, key):<br>        &quot;&quot;&quot;Gets the node responsible for the given key.&quot;&quot;&quot;<br>        if not self.ring:<br>            return None<br>        hashed_key = self._hash(key)<br>        pos = self.ring.bisect_left(hashed_key)<br>        if pos == len(self.ring):<br>            pos = 0<br>        return self.ring.peekitem(pos)[1]<br>ch = ConsistentHashing()<br># Add nodes<br>ch.add_node(&quot;db1&quot;)<br>ch.add_node(&quot;db2&quot;)<br>ch.add_node(&quot;db3&quot;)<br># Assign keys<br>keys = [&quot;user1&quot;, &quot;user2&quot;, &quot;user3&quot;]<br>for key in keys:<br>    print(f&quot;{key} is stored in {ch.get_node(key)}&quot;)<br># Add a new node<br>print(&quot;\\nAdding db4...&quot;)<br>ch.add_node(&quot;db4&quot;)<br>for key in keys:<br>    print(f&quot;{key} is stored in {ch.get_node(key)}&quot;)<br># Remove a node<br>print(&quot;\\nRemoving db2...&quot;)<br>ch.remove_node(&quot;db2&quot;)<br>for key in keys:<br>    print(f&quot;{key} is stored in {ch.get_node(key)}&quot;)<\/pre><h3>Key Takeaways<\/h3><ol><li><strong>Significant Reduction in Data Movement<\/strong>: Consistent hashing minimizes remapping, significantly reducing the overhead of adding or removing nodes.<\/li><li><strong>Load Balancing<\/strong>: By using virtual nodes, consistent hashing ensures an even distribution of keys across nodes.<\/li><li><strong>Scalability<\/strong>: This technique is ideal for scaling distributed systems with minimal disruptions.<\/li><\/ol><h3>Conclusion<\/h3><p>Consistent hashing is not a silver bullet, but it’s an invaluable tool for managing distributed systems. While it doesn’t eliminate data remapping entirely, it drastically reduces the impact, enabling systems to scale efficiently. By implementing consistent hashing, you can build resilient and scalable architectures capable of handling billions of records with ease.<\/p>"},{"slug":"hidden-mongodb-instances-essential-for-reliable-voting-and-deadlock-preven","title":"Hidden MongoDB Instances: Essential for Reliable Voting and Deadlock Prevention","date":"2024-11-22","dateLabel":"Nov 22, 2024","topic":"MongoDB","tags":["nosql","database","mongodb","replication"],"excerpt":"MongoDB is one of the most popular NoSQL databases, known for its replica set architecture, which ensures high availability and fault tolerance. Among the members of a replica set, hidden...","readMinutes":4,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/hidden-mongodb-instances-essential-for-reliable-voting-and-deadlock-prevention-dbc3eb079bf4","image":"","content":"<p>MongoDB is one of the most popular NoSQL databases, known for its replica set architecture, which ensures high availability and fault tolerance. Among the members of a replica set, hidden instances play a crucial role in maintaining operational stability. In this blog, we’ll explore why hidden instances should participate in voting, how they help prevent deadlocks, the benefits they provide, and the potential drawbacks to consider.<\/p><h3>What Is a Hidden MongoDB Instance?<\/h3><p>A hidden MongoDB instance is a member of a replica set configured with hidden: true. Hidden nodes:<\/p><ul><li>Do not accept client reads or writes directly.<\/li><li>Are invisible to applications (not included in replica set connection strings).<\/li><li>Do not participate in elections as primary (priority set to 0).<\/li><\/ul><p>Hidden instances are often used for specialised purposes like reporting, backups, or disaster recovery, as they replicate all data but remain isolated from application workloads.<\/p><h3>Why Hidden Instances Should Vote<\/h3><p>Hidden instances can be configured to participate in voting. By default, a replica set can have up to 7 voting members, and hidden nodes can contribute to this quorum. Here’s why enabling voting for hidden nodes is a good practice:<\/p><h4>1. Avoiding Deadlocks During Elections<\/h4><p>In replica sets, elections occur when the primary node fails. To elect a new primary, the majority of voting members must agree.<\/p><ul><li>Problem: If the number of voting members is insufficient or uneven, elections may stall, causing a deadlock that halts database operations.<\/li><li>Solution: Hidden nodes contribute to the voting process, ensuring a clear majority and preventing election deadlocks.<\/li><\/ul><h4>2. Increased Redundancy in Voting<\/h4><p>When other nodes are offline or lagging significantly, hidden members provide additional votes, increasing the likelihood of successful elections. This is particularly useful in scenarios where network partitions occur or nodes experience temporary issues.<\/p><h4>3. Maintaining Availability in Edge Cases<\/h4><p>Hidden instances, while not involved in application-facing workloads, can step in to ensure critical functions like elections proceed smoothly. This makes the system more resilient against unexpected failures.<\/p><h3>Benefits of Hidden Instances Voting<\/h3><ol><li>Better Fault Tolerance:<br>Hidden nodes enhance fault tolerance by acting as reliable voters, ensuring the cluster remains operational during partial outages.<\/li><li>Optimized Load Management:<br>They allow operational flexibility without impacting read or write performance, especially for use cases like reporting or analytics.<\/li><li>Disaster Recovery:<br>Hidden nodes often reside in a different data center. Voting rights enable them to contribute to quorum even if a data center experiences an outage.<\/li><li>Reliable Replication:<br>Hidden nodes can replicate data without interrupting critical operations, ensuring consistency while supporting the voting process.<\/li><\/ol><h3>Potential Drawbacks of Hidden Instances Voting<\/h3><p>While voting by hidden instances has clear advantages, there are some trade-offs to consider:<\/p><h4>1. Network Overhead<\/h4><p>Adding more voting members increases the amount of data exchanged during elections. In scenarios with high network latency, this can slightly delay election completion.<\/p><h4>2. Configuration Complexity<\/h4><p>Configuring hidden members to vote requires careful planning. Misconfiguration can lead to unexpected issues, such as voting members being located in a single availability zone, increasing the risk of quorum failure.<\/p><h4>3. Limited Use Cases<\/h4><p>For systems with minimal workloads or low fault tolerance requirements, hidden voting members may not be necessary and could introduce unnecessary complexity.<\/p><h4>4. Resource Utilization<\/h4><p>While hidden nodes don’t handle client traffic, they still consume resources like CPU, memory, and storage. If not managed properly, this could strain the infrastructure without delivering proportional benefits.<\/p><h3>Best Practices for Configuring Hidden Instances<\/h3><p>To maximize the benefits of hidden voting members while minimizing risks:<\/p><ul><li>Set priority: 0 to prevent hidden nodes from becoming the primary.<\/li><li>Use hidden nodes for specific workloads like backups and analytics to keep application-facing nodes optimized.<\/li><li>Distribute hidden nodes across different data centers for redundancy.<\/li><li>Limit the total number of voting members to avoid exceeding the recommended cap of 7.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Conclusion<\/h3><p>Hidden MongoDB instances are more than just passive members of a replica set. By allowing them to vote, you enhance the resilience and reliability of your cluster, ensuring elections proceed smoothly and deadlocks are avoided. While there are some trade-offs, the benefits of improved fault tolerance, high availability, and operational flexibility often outweigh the drawbacks.<\/p><p>For systems requiring robust fault tolerance, especially in distributed environments, hidden voting members are a critical part of the architecture. Proper planning and configuration can help you harness their full potential while keeping your replica set efficient and reliable.<\/p>"},{"slug":"how-mongodb-loses-availability-in-the-cap-theorem","title":"How MongoDB Loses Availability in the CAP Theorem","date":"2024-10-30","dateLabel":"Oct 30, 2024","topic":"MongoDB","tags":["engineering","nosql","cap","mongodb","database"],"excerpt":"In the world of distributed databases, every system design must account for trade-offs between Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance, as outlined by the CAP theorem. This fou...","readMinutes":5,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/how-mongodb-loses-availability-in-the-cap-theorem-79735e4dc797","image":"","content":"<p>In the world of distributed databases, every system design must account for trade-offs between Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance, as outlined by the CAP theorem. This foundational principle dictates that a distributed system can only fulfill two of these three guarantees simultaneously. MongoDB, a popular NoSQL database, prioritizes Consistency and Partition Tolerance (CP), which inherently leads to a loss in Availability. In this blog, we’ll explore why MongoDB makes this choice, how it impacts Availability, and how it affects real-world applications.<\/p><h4>The CAP Theorem Explained<\/h4><p>The CAP theorem, introduced by Eric Brewer, suggests that in a distributed system, you can only achieve two of the following three properties:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Consistency<\/strong>: Ensures every read reflects the most recent write.<\/li><li><strong>Availability<\/strong>: Guarantees that every request receives a response, though it may not reflect the latest data.<\/li><li><strong>Partition Tolerance<\/strong>: Allows the system to continue functioning despite network partitions.<\/li><\/ul><p>MongoDB’s architecture prioritizes Consistency and Partition Tolerance, which means it is willing to lose Availability during certain scenarios to maintain data integrity. Let’s dive into how and why MongoDB makes this trade-off.<\/p><h4>How MongoDB Loses Availability: A Deep Dive<\/h4><p>MongoDB’s commitment to maintaining a consistent, partition-tolerant system inherently impacts its Availability. Here are the main ways this trade-off manifests:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Dependency on Primary Nodes and Failover Delays:<br><\/strong>a. MongoDB’s architecture revolves around <em>replica sets<\/em> consisting of a single primary node and multiple secondary nodes. The primary node handles all writes to maintain data consistency across replicas, while secondaries replicate data changes.<br>b. When a primary node fails, MongoDB initiates an election to promote a secondary to primary. During this election, the database halts all write operations and potentially some read operations. This results in temporary unavailability until a new primary is elected, which may take seconds to minutes depending on the configuration. MongoDB’s need to uphold consistency leads it to sacrifice Availability during this failover period.<\/li><li><strong>Partition Tolerance Over Availability in Network Disruptions<br><\/strong>a. Distributed systems are susceptible to network partitions, where some nodes can’t communicate with others. MongoDB, being partition-tolerant, chooses to limit Availability instead of risking data inconsistency. If the primary node becomes isolated and can’t communicate with a majority of the replicas, it steps down and stops processing requests, making the database temporarily unavailable.<br>b. This behavior prevents “split-brain” scenarios where two primary nodes could accept writes independently, leading to data divergence. By prioritizing Partition Tolerance, MongoDB may lose Availability during network partitions to ensure that data remains consistent across replicas.<\/li><li><strong>Impact of Write and Read Concerns on Availability:<br><\/strong>a. MongoDB offers different <em>write concerns<\/em>, which let users specify how strictly writes are acknowledged across replicas. A higher write concern level (e.g., requiring acknowledgment from multiple nodes) can lead to reduced Availability. If some replicas are down or unreachable, MongoDB will reject the write request to maintain consistency. This safeguard can impact Availability, especially in unstable networks or large-scale deployments with frequent replica issues.<br>b. Similarly, MongoDB’s <em>read concerns<\/em> allow users to control data freshness in reads. The “linearizable” read concern guarantees the latest data, but it can make reads temporarily unavailable if the primary is unreachable.<\/li><\/ol><h4>Real-World Scenarios: How MongoDB’s CP Focus Impacts Availability<\/h4><p>The loss of Availability in MongoDB’s CP-focused design can have notable effects on applications, especially those requiring high uptime. Here are some examples:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Financial Transactions<\/strong>: Banks and financial institutions need data to be accurate and consistent. MongoDB’s ability to maintain CP makes it a good fit for these cases, but at the cost of short unavailability during failovers or network partitions. For example, during a primary election, transactional systems might temporarily halt to prevent inconsistent balances from being recorded.<\/li><li><strong>Inventory Management in E-commerce<\/strong>: E-commerce applications rely on accurate stock counts to manage customer orders. MongoDB’s design ensures that stock data remains consistent, but during network issues or primary failover, the system may become momentarily unavailable. This unavailability could prevent customers from completing orders or viewing accurate inventory levels, impacting the shopping experience.<\/li><\/ul><h4>When to Choose MongoDB Despite Availability Losses<\/h4><p>MongoDB’s trade-off between Consistency and Availability is ideal for applications where data consistency is more important than total uptime. Here’s a summary to guide you:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Consider MongoDB for Applications Requiring High Data Integrity<\/strong>: Use MongoDB for applications where consistency is critical and where brief unavailability won’t cause significant disruption. Examples include finance, inventory management, and other areas where incorrect data could lead to critical issues.<\/li><li><strong>Avoid MongoDB for High-Availability, Eventual Consistency Needs<\/strong>: For applications that can tolerate eventual consistency (where slightly outdated data is acceptable) but require high availability, consider alternatives like Cassandra or DynamoDB, which favor Availability and Partition Tolerance (AP).<\/li><\/ul><h4>Conclusion<\/h4><p>MongoDB’s design aligns with the CAP theorem’s constraints by prioritizing Consistency and Partition Tolerance over Availability. This trade-off enables MongoDB to prevent data inconsistencies at the cost of occasional unavailability, making it a strong choice for applications requiring data accuracy. However, developers should weigh this against the availability needs of their specific application.<\/p><p>Understanding MongoDB’s approach to the CAP theorem helps ensure you’re choosing the right database architecture for your requirements. Whether Availability, Consistency, or Partition Tolerance is most critical to your application will guide your decision — ultimately enabling you to harness MongoDB’s strengths or choose an alternative that better fits your needs.<\/p><p>By breaking down these scenarios, we see how MongoDB’s focus on Consistency and Partition Tolerance shapes its approach to Availability, making it a powerful but specialized tool in the world of distributed databases.<\/p>"},{"slug":"the-cap-theorem-explained-choosing-the-right-database-for-your-needs","title":"The CAP Theorem Explained: Choosing the Right Database for Your Needs","date":"2024-10-18","dateLabel":"Oct 18, 2024","topic":"Distributed Systems","tags":["mongodb","database","engineering","mysql","cap-theorem"],"excerpt":"In distributed systems, achieving Consistency `C` , Availability `A` , and Partition Tolerance `P` simultaneously is impossible. As a developer, it’s essential to understand the trade-off...","readMinutes":4,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/the-cap-theorem-explained-choosing-the-right-database-for-your-needs-17111941e9e3","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1003/1*dP-jeueg4XyppHeLGNgbrA.png","content":"<p>In distributed systems, achieving <strong>Consistency `C`<\/strong>, <strong>Availability `A`<\/strong>, and <strong>Partition Tolerance `P`<\/strong> simultaneously is impossible. As a developer, it’s essential to understand the trade-offs of each database and decide which properties are most critical for your application.<\/p><p><strong>What Is the CAP Theorem?<\/strong><\/p><p>The CAP Theorem states that a distributed database system can only provide two out of the following three guarantees at any given time:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Consistency `C`<\/strong>: Every read receives the most recent write or an error. All nodes see the same data at the same time, ensuring data correctness.<\/li><li><strong>Availability `A`<\/strong>: Every request receives a (non-error) response. The system remains operational even if some nodes fail.<\/li><li><strong>Partition Tolerance `P`<\/strong>: The system continues to operate even if partitions or communication breakdowns occur between nodes in a distributed system.<\/li><\/ol><p>Let’s explore the CAP theorem and look at specific examples of popular databases, focusing on which combination of <strong>C<\/strong>, <strong>A<\/strong>, or <strong>P<\/strong> they prioritize.<\/p><h3>1. CP (Consistency and Partition Tolerance) Databases<\/h3><p><strong>CP systems<\/strong> prioritize consistency and partition tolerance. These systems guarantee that all nodes reflect the same data after a partition, but they might sacrifice availability in case of a partitions.<\/p><h4>MongoDB (in specific configurations)<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> NoSQL (Document-based)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> CP (with strong consistency settings)<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> MongoDB, when configured for <strong>strong consistency<\/strong> (using a replica set with a majority write concern), prioritizes consistency. In the event of a partition, MongoDB may reject reads or writes to maintain consistent data, sacrificing availability.<\/li><\/ul><h4>HBase<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> NoSQL (Column-family based)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> CP<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> HBase ensures strong consistency but may become unavailable during partitions. It uses a master-slave architecture that favors consistency, even if that means rejecting some requests.<\/li><\/ul><h4>Cockroach DB<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> NewSQL (Relational)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> CP<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> Cockroach DB aims to provide SQL consistency in a distributed environment. It’s designed to tolerate partitions and maintain consistency, but at the cost of availability if partitions happen.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When to use CP systems?<\/strong> When your application demands data accuracy at all times, like in financial services, banking, or e-commerce order processing.<\/p><h3>2. CA (Consistency and Availability) Databases<\/h3><p><strong>CA systems<\/strong> maintain consistency and availability, but they don’t tolerate partitions well. These databases work well in environments where partitions are either rare or non-existent.<\/p><h4>MySQL (in a single-node setup)<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> Relational (SQL)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> CA<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> In single-node configurations, MySQL maintains strong consistency and is always available as long as the system itself remains operational. However, it doesn’t handle partitions because it’s not distributed in this setup.<\/li><\/ul><h4>PostgreSQL (single-node configuration)<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> Relational (SQL)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> CA<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> Similar to MySQL, PostgreSQL ensures consistency and availability as long as there are no partitions. It’s ideal for environments where you don’t expect partitions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When to use CA systems?<\/strong> When your system can assume network reliability or when you’re operating in a controlled environment with no partition tolerance requirements. It’s well-suited for mission-critical applications like financial systems or enterprise environments.<\/p><h3>3. AP (Availability and Partition Tolerance) Databases<\/h3><p><strong>AP systems<\/strong> prioritize availability and partition tolerance, meaning they will remain operational even in the face of partitions. However, they may sacrifice consistency, returning stale or conflicting data.<\/p><h4>Cassandra<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> NoSQL (Column-family based)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> AP<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> Cassandra prioritizes availability and partition tolerance. It’s highly resilient to network issues, but it allows for eventual consistency, meaning that during a partition, different nodes may show different versions of the same data until the system converges.<\/li><\/ul><h4>Amazon DynamoDB<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> NoSQL (Key-value)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> AP<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> DynamoDB favors availability and partition tolerance. It uses eventual consistency as its default but allows developers to choose between strongly consistent reads and eventually consistent reads. During a partition, DynamoDB remains operational but may serve outdated data.<\/li><\/ul><h4>Riak<\/h4><ul><li><strong>Type:<\/strong> NoSQL (Key-value)<\/li><li><strong>CAP Preferences:<\/strong> AP<\/li><li><strong>Why?<\/strong> Similar to Cassandra and DynamoDB, Riak focuses on keeping the system available during partitions. It may allow conflicting data to exist temporarily but ensures that the system doesn’t go down.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When to use AP systems?<\/strong> When your application needs to be highly available and can tolerate eventual consistency, like social media platforms, real-time messaging systems, and logging services.<\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1003/1*dP-jeueg4XyppHeLGNgbrA.png\" /><\/figure><h3>Conclusion<\/h3><p>The <strong>CAP theorem<\/strong> forces you to make tough decisions when building distributed systems. You can’t achieve <strong>Consistency<\/strong>, <strong>Availability<\/strong>, and <strong>Partition Tolerance<\/strong> all at once, so it’s important to consider your application’s needs. For example:<\/p><ul><li>Use <strong>CP systems<\/strong> when consistency is paramount, like in financial services.<\/li><li>Use <strong>CA systems<\/strong> in scenarios where partitions are rare, and you need both consistency and availability, such as small-scale web apps.<\/li><li>Use <strong>AP systems<\/strong> when you can tolerate eventual consistency but must ensure high availability, like in social media platforms or logging systems.<\/li><\/ul><p>Ultimately, understanding the CAP theorem will help you make informed decisions about which databases and architectures best suit your application’s unique requirements.<\/p><p>Stay tuned, where I’ll explain how each database compromises one of the CAP principles and the implications of these trade-offs.<\/p>"},{"slug":"understanding-sparse-indexes-in-mongodb-use-cases-pros-and-cons-and-query","title":"Understanding Sparse Indexes in MongoDB: Use Cases, Pros and Cons, and Query Behavior","date":"2024-09-29","dateLabel":"Sep 29, 2024","topic":"MongoDB","tags":["index","sparse-index","mongodb","database","sparse"],"excerpt":"MongoDB is a powerful NoSQL database that offers flexible data modeling capabilities. One of its notable features is the ability to create various types of indexes to optimize query perfo...","readMinutes":5,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/understanding-sparse-indexes-in-mongodb-use-cases-pros-and-cons-and-query-behavior-8bcbb0337431","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/445/0*XCbZTLDJ4PP78sAO","content":"<figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/445/0*XCbZTLDJ4PP78sAO\" /><\/figure><p>MongoDB is a powerful NoSQL database that offers flexible data modeling capabilities. One of its notable features is the ability to create various types of indexes to optimize query performance. Among these, sparse indexes stand out for their efficiency in handling optional fields. In this blog, we’ll explore what sparse indexes are, how and when to use them, their pros and cons, and important considerations regarding their interaction with queries and sorting, including sparse compound indexes.<\/p><h3>What is a Sparse Index?<\/h3><p>A sparse index in MongoDB is an index that only includes documents containing a specific field. If a document does not have that field, it will not be included in the index. This makes sparse indexes particularly useful for collections where some documents may not have all fields defined.<\/p><h3>When to Use Sparse Indexes<\/h3><p>Sparse indexes are ideal for:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Optional Fields<\/strong>: When documents in a collection have fields that are not present in every document. For example, in a user profiles collection, only some users might have a “bio” field.<\/li><li><strong>Improved Performance<\/strong>: If you frequently query for documents based on specific field values, a sparse index can enhance performance by minimizing the number of documents that need to be scanned.<\/li><li><strong>Lower Storage Costs<\/strong>: Sparse indexes take up less space than regular indexes since they only include documents with the indexed field, leading to more efficient use of storage.<\/li><\/ol><h3>How to Create a Sparse Index<\/h3><p>Creating a sparse index in MongoDB is straightforward. You can use the createIndex method with the { sparse: true } option. Here’s an example:<\/p><pre>db.users.createIndex({ bio: 1 }, { sparse: true });<\/pre><p>In this example, we’re creating a sparse index on the bio field in the users collection.<\/p><h3>Sparse Compound Indexes<\/h3><p>In addition to single-field sparse indexes, MongoDB also supports sparse compound indexes, which are indexes on multiple fields. A compound sparse index includes only the documents that have all the fields specified in the index. This is useful for optimizing queries that filter on multiple optional fields.<\/p><h4>Example of Sparse Compound Index<\/h4><p>Suppose we have a collection of user profiles that includes both bio and age as optional fields:<\/p><pre>{ &quot;_id&quot;: 1, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;, &quot;bio&quot;: &quot;Loves painting&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 30 }<br>{ &quot;_id&quot;: 2, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Bob&quot; }<br>{ &quot;_id&quot;: 3, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Charlie&quot;, &quot;bio&quot;: &quot;Enjoys hiking&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 25 }<br>{ &quot;_id&quot;: 4, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;David&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 40 }<\/pre><p>You can create a sparse compound index on both the bio and age fields:<\/p><pre>db.users.createIndex({ bio: 1, age: 1 }, { sparse: true });<\/pre><p>This index will only include documents that have both the bio and age fields, optimizing queries that search for users with both attributes.<\/p><h3>Pros and Cons of Sparse Indexes<\/h3><h4>Pros<\/h4><ol><li><strong>Efficiency<\/strong>: Sparse indexes reduce the size of the index, leading to faster query performance for documents that contain the indexed fields.<\/li><li><strong>Storage Savings<\/strong>: They consume less disk space compared to regular indexes since they only index a subset of documents.<\/li><li><strong>Flexibility<\/strong>: Sparse indexes allow for indexing fields that may be absent in many documents without the overhead of including all documents.<\/li><\/ol><h4>Cons<\/h4><ol><li><strong>Limited Use Cases<\/strong>: Sparse indexes can only be used for queries that specifically target the indexed fields. Queries that do not include these fields will not utilize the index.<\/li><li><strong>Potential for Confusion<\/strong>: Developers new to MongoDB might overlook the behavior of sparse indexes, leading to unexpected results when querying.<\/li><li><strong>Indexing Overhead<\/strong>: While they reduce size, creating and maintaining indexes incurs overhead. If many documents later include the indexed fields, the index may need to be rebuilt.<\/li><li><strong>Performance Impact on Writes<\/strong>: Sparse indexes can impact write performance. The index must be maintained as documents are added or modified, so having too many sparse indexes may slow down write operations.<\/li><li><strong>Uniqueness Considerations<\/strong>: Sparse indexes do not guarantee uniqueness for fields that are not present in all documents. To enforce uniqueness, you must explicitly define the index as unique:<\/li><\/ol><pre>db.users.createIndex({ bio: 1 }, { sparse: true, unique: true });<\/pre><h3>Important Consideration: Query Behavior with Sorting<\/h3><p>One critical aspect to note is that MongoDB’s query planner may not use a sparse index for sorting operations unless you explicitly provide a hint.<\/p><h4>Example Scenario<\/h4><p>Consider a collection of user profiles as defined above. If you create a sparse index on bio and age:<\/p><pre>db.users.createIndex({ bio: 1, age: 1 }, { sparse: true });<\/pre><p>Now, if you run a query to find users with a bio and sort by name:<\/p><pre>db.users.find({ bio: { $exists: true } }).sort({ name: 1 });<\/pre><p>MongoDB may not use the sparse index for sorting since it does not include documents without the bio field, potentially leading to a collection scan instead.<\/p><h4>Forcing the Use of Sparse Index with Hint<\/h4><p>To ensure that the sparse index is used, you can provide a hint:<\/p><pre>db.users.find({ bio: { $exists: true } })<br>         .sort({ name: 1 })<br>         .hint({ bio: 1, age: 1 });<\/pre><p>This explicitly directs MongoDB to use the sparse compound index on the bio and age fields, ensuring optimal performance for your query.<\/p><h3>Conclusion<\/h3><p>Sparse indexes, including sparse compound indexes, are a powerful feature in MongoDB that can optimize performance and reduce storage costs, especially for collections with optional fields. However, understanding their limitations, particularly in relation to sorting and other query behaviors, is crucial for effective database management. By leveraging sparse indexes wisely and being mindful of their nuances, you can significantly enhance the efficiency of your MongoDB applications.<\/p>"},{"slug":"optimizing-mongodb-with-projections-beyond-just-saving-network-time","title":"Optimizing MongoDB with Projections: Beyond Just Saving Network Time","date":"2024-09-16","dateLabel":"Sep 16, 2024","topic":"MongoDB","tags":["engineering","mongo","projection","mongodb","nosql"],"excerpt":"Introduction When working with MongoDB, performance optimization is always a key concern — especially when dealing with large datasets or high-latency networks. One powerful feature Mongo...","readMinutes":5,"originalUrl":"https://hansajdeghun.medium.com/optimizing-mongodb-with-projections-beyond-just-saving-network-time-5503fba726ed","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/433/1*-5FQF5PfcJVxwfatzHBYdw.png","content":"<h3>Introduction<\/h3><p>When working with MongoDB, performance optimization is always a key concern — especially when dealing with large datasets or high-latency networks. One powerful feature MongoDB offers to improve performance is <strong>projections<\/strong>. Projections allow you to select specific fields to include or exclude in your query results, reducing the size of the data returned. While projections are primarily known for saving network time, their benefits extend beyond just reducing data transfer. This blog dives into the full impact of projections, how they can be used, and the role they play in optimizing your MongoDB queries.<\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/433/1*-5FQF5PfcJVxwfatzHBYdw.png\" /><\/figure><h3>What Are Projections in MongoDB?<\/h3><p>Projections in MongoDB allow you to control which fields of a document are returned by a query. Instead of fetching the entire document, you can specify only the fields you need, reducing the amount of data transferred between MongoDB and your application.<\/p><pre>// Example of using a projection to include only &#39;name&#39; and &#39;age&#39;, and exclude &#39;_id&#39;:<br>db.collection.find(<br>   { age: { $gt: 18 } }, <br>   { name: 1, age: 1, _id: 0 }<br>)<\/pre><p>This query retrieves only the name and age fields for documents where age is greater than 18, excluding the _id field.<\/p><h3>Why Projections Matter: More Than Just Network Optimization<\/h3><h4>1. Primary Benefit: Reduce Network Traffic<\/h4><p>The most apparent advantage of projections is reducing the size of data sent over the network. This is especially useful when:<\/p><ul><li>You are dealing with large documents where you only need a subset of the fields.<\/li><li>Network latency or bandwidth is a bottleneck, and minimizing the data payload improves responsiveness.<\/li><\/ul><p>For example, imagine fetching only user names and email addresses from a collection containing user profiles. If the documents contain extensive data, such as addresses, payment information, and purchase history, using projections to select just the name and email fields can significantly decrease the size of the result set.<\/p><h4>2. Improve Application Processing Efficiency<\/h4><p>In addition to saving network time, projections can enhance the performance of your application by reducing the amount of data that needs to be processed. When the application receives only the fields it requires, it can handle the data faster and more efficiently.<\/p><p>This is particularly beneficial for applications running on resource-constrained environments, where memory usage and processing overhead are critical considerations.<\/p><h4>3. Save Client-Side Resources (Memory and I/O)<\/h4><p>When only relevant fields are returned, the application saves on memory consumption and input/output operations. This is important in situations where the volume of data transferred from MongoDB to the client is high, but only a small portion is needed for the actual computation or display.<\/p><h3>Projections and Query Performance: Can They Speed Up MongoDB Queries?<\/h3><p>While projections don’t directly speed up the process of finding the data, they can have a noticeable impact on query performance under specific conditions. Here’s how:<\/p><h4>1. Index-Only or Covered Queries<\/h4><p>One of the most significant performance benefits of using projections comes from <strong>covered queries<\/strong>. A query is covered when MongoDB can return results directly from an index without needing to scan the full documents in the collection. If the fields used in the query and the projection are part of an index, MongoDB can serve the query entirely from the index.<\/p><p><strong>Example of a covered query<\/strong>:<\/p><pre>// Create an index on the &#39;name&#39; and &#39;age&#39; fields<br>db.collection.createIndex({ name: 1, age: 1 });<\/pre><pre>// Query using both indexed fields with a projection<br>db.collection.find({ age: { $gt: 18 } }, { name: 1, age: 1, _id: 0 });<\/pre><p>In this case, MongoDB retrieves the result directly from the index, without scanning the actual documents. This reduces I/O operations and improves query performance.<\/p><h4>2. Optimizing Query Execution with Projections<\/h4><p>Although projections alone don’t optimize query execution, combining them with properly indexed fields can lead to faster queries. You should always analyze your queries using MongoDB’s explain() method to ensure that projections are working in harmony with your indexes.<\/p><pre>db.collection.find({ age: { $gt: 18 } }, { name: 1, age: 1, _id: 0 }).explain();<\/pre><h3>What Projections Don’t Do<\/h3><p>While projections have many advantages, it’s important to understand their limitations:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Projections Don’t Improve Document Search Time<\/strong>: Projections only reduce the size of the result set returned. They don’t make MongoDB locate documents faster. For that, indexing is essential.<\/li><li><strong>Not a Replacement for Indexing<\/strong>: Without proper indexes, MongoDB still needs to scan the documents to find the results, and projections won’t speed up this process.<\/li><\/ul><h3>Use Case: Where Projections Shine<\/h3><p>Consider a scenario where an e-commerce application needs to display order information. Each order document may include dozens of fields, such as customer details, shipping information, order items, payment history, and more. However, to display a basic order list, you might only need the order ID, date, and total amount.<\/p><p>Using projections, you can retrieve just those three fields, reducing both the network traffic and the processing time for your application. This not only improves the app’s responsiveness but also reduces server and client-side resource consumption.<\/p><pre>db.orders.find(<br>   { status: &quot;shipped&quot; }, <br>   { orderID: 1, orderDate: 1, totalAmount: 1, _id: 0 }<br>);<\/pre><h3>Conclusion<\/h3><p>Projections in MongoDB offer an easy yet powerful way to optimize your queries. While their primary benefit is reducing network traffic, their impact extends beyond that:<\/p><ul><li>They help improve application efficiency by reducing the amount of data your app needs to handle.<\/li><li>In specific cases, they enable covered queries, which can significantly improve query execution times.<\/li><li>However, they don’t replace the need for proper indexing and query optimization.<\/li><\/ul><p>By using projections thoughtfully, you can save both network and processing time, ultimately making your MongoDB-powered applications more responsive and efficient.<\/p><h3>Bonus Tip: Combine Projections with Indexing<\/h3><p>To maximize the performance gains from projections, always combine them with proper indexing. Use the explain() method to analyze your queries and ensure that MongoDB is using indexes optimally.<\/p>"},{"slug":"zero-downtime-mysql-migration","title":"Zero Downtime MySQL Migration","date":"2024-06-09","dateLabel":"Jun 9, 2024","topic":"Databases","tags":["triggers-in-mysql","zero-downtime","engineering","mysql","tech"],"excerpt":"Using a Shadow Table and Triggers for 1 Billion+ Rows Migrating a massive MySQL table with over a billion rows while ensuring zero downtime can be a complex task. However, using a shadow...","readMinutes":4,"originalUrl":"https://medium.com/@hansrajdeghun/zero-downtime-mysql-migration-91fca09d6973","image":"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/0*3bP6OmL8t61mwhms","content":"<h3>Using a Shadow Table and Triggers for 1 Billion+ Rows<\/h3><p>Migrating a massive MySQL table with over a billion rows while ensuring zero downtime can be a complex task. However, using a shadow tables and triggers, you can perform this migration seamlessly. In this blog post, we’ll walk you through the process with a practical example, helping you achieve a smooth migration without affecting your live environment.<\/p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/1024/0*3bP6OmL8t61mwhms\" /><\/figure><h3>Why Zero Downtime Migration?<\/h3><p>Zero downtime migration is crucial for applications requiring high availability. Downtime can result in lost revenue, poor user experience, and brand damage. By using a shadow table and triggers, you can migrate large datasets without interrupting your service.<\/p><h3>Example Scenario<\/h3><p>Suppose you have an e-commerce application with a large orders table that you need to migrate. The orders table has the following schema:<\/p><pre>CREATE TABLE orders (<br>    order_id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,<br>    customer_id BIGINT,<br>    product_id BIGINT,<br>    order_date DATETIME,<br>    status VARCHAR(20),<br>    amount DECIMAL(10, 2)<br>);<\/pre><p>Your goal is to migrate this table to a new schema without downtime.<\/p><h3>Step-by-Step Guide to Zero Downtime Migration<\/h3><h4>1. Preparation<\/h4><h4>Start with a comprehensive plan:<\/h4><ul><li>Define the scope and objectives.<\/li><li>Analyse the current schema and data volume.<\/li><li>Identify potential bottlenecks and challenges.<\/li><\/ul><blockquote><strong>Ensure Data Consistency<\/strong><br>Ensure your data is consistent and that you have recent backups to prevent data loss and discrepancies during the migration process.<\/blockquote><h4>2. Create the Shadow Table<\/h4><p>Create a new table (orders_new) that matches the schema of your original table.<\/p><pre>CREATE TABLE orders_new LIKE orders;<br>ALTER TABLE orders_new<br>ADD INDEX idx_customer_id (customer_id);<\/pre><h4>3. Implement Triggers for Real-Time Sync<\/h4><p>To keep your data synchronized, create triggers on the original orders table. These triggers will capture INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations, applying them to the shadow table.<\/p><h4>a. INSERT Trigger<\/h4><pre>CREATE TRIGGER orders_after_insert<br>AFTER INSERT ON orders <br>FOR EACH ROW <br>BEGIN<br>INSERT INTO orders_new SET<br>        order_id = NEW.order_id,<br>        customer_id = NEW.customer_id,<br>        product_id = NEW.product_id,<br>        order_date = NEW.order_date,<br>        status = NEW.status,<br>        amount = NEW.amount;END;<\/pre><p><strong>b. UPDATE Trigger<\/strong><\/p><pre>CREATE TRIGGER orders_after_update<br>AFTER UPDATE ON orders<br>FOR EACH ROW<br>BEGIN<br>UPDATE orders_new SET<br>        customer_id = NEW.customer_id,<br>        product_id = NEW.product_id,<br>        order_date = NEW.order_date,<br>        status = NEW.status,<br>        amount = NEW.amount<br>    WHERE order_id = NEW.order_id;END;<\/pre><p><strong>c. DELETE Trigger<\/strong><\/p><pre>CREATE TRIGGER orders_after_delete<br>AFTER DELETE ON orders<br>FOR EACH ROW<br>BEGIN<br>DELETE FROM orders_new WHERE order_id = OLD.order_id;END;<\/pre><h4>4. Initial Data Migration<\/h4><p>Use a script to copy the existing data from the orders table to the orders_new table in batches. This minimizes the load on your database and avoids locking issues.<\/p><pre>INSERT INTO orders_new SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 10000;<\/pre><p>Repeat this process until all data has been copied. Use a script or a tool to automate this in chunks:<\/p><pre>-- Example script logic in pseudocode<br>DECLARE @last_id BIGINT = 0;<br>DECLARE @batch_size INT = 10000;<br>WHILE (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE order_id &gt; @last_id) &gt; 0<br>BEGIN<br>INSERT INTO orders_new<br>        SELECT * FROM orders<br>        WHERE order_id &gt; @last_id<br>ORDER BY order_id<br>        LIMIT @batch_size;<br>    SET @last_id = (SELECT MAX(order_id) FROM orders_new);END;<\/pre><h4>5. Verify Data Consistency<\/h4><p>Once the initial data migration is complete and the triggers are in place, verify data consistency between the two tables. Ensure both tables have the same data.<\/p><pre>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders;SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders_new;<br>-- Check sums of critical columns to ensure data integrity<br>SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders;<br>SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders_new;<\/pre><h4>6. Switch Over<\/h4><p>When you are confident that the orders_new table is fully synchronized with the orders table, update your application to point to the new table. This switch should be quick and without downtime if done correctly.<\/p><pre>RENAME TABLE orders TO orders_backup, orders_new TO orders;<\/pre><p>This renaming process is atomic in MySQL, ensuring no data is lost during the switch.<\/p><h4>7. Monitor and Cleanup<\/h4><p>After the switch, monitor your application closely for any issues. If everything is working fine, you can drop the old table (now renamed orders_backup) after a safe period.<\/p><pre>DROP TABLE orders_backup;<\/pre><h3>Conclusion<\/h3><p>Migrating a massive MySQL table with zero downtime is challenging but achievable with the right approach. By using a shadow table and triggers, you can ensure a seamless transition with minimal impact on your application’s availability. Planning, careful execution, and thorough testing are key to a successful migration.<\/p><p>This method allows you to handle even the largest tables efficiently, keeping your application up and running throughout the migration process. Happy migrating!<\/p>"}];
